     <p>In Spain, the deduction for a similar tax paid abroad applies only where the tax is charged by personal obligation (Law 29/1987, article 23.1); according to the Tax Agency, that means heirs habitually resident in Spain at the date of death. A non-resident heir pays in Spain on what is in Spain; whether the country of residence relieves the double charge depends on that country's law or on a treaty. Spain has a treaty with France that covers inheritance tax (published in the Spanish Official Gazette on 7 January 1964): real estate is taxed only in the State where it is located (article 30), and intangible assets not covered by the rules for business and professional assets (articles 31 and 32), which include securities and other claims, only in the State where the deceased was resident at the time of death (article 34); patents, trademarks and intellectual property have their own rules. So, in an inheritance involving two countries, the law of both is checked before deciding where each asset is declared.</p>
 
